A||N||6||Predator||Hypertech Fan switch||||5||
Z||000000||Predator||09-02-2000||07:42 PM||||Hey guys, <BR>Can someone who has installed the 176 degree Hypertech fan switch tell me how to splice it into my wires that exist. The instructions I got with the switch is for 1 wire and I have 2 wires going to my current switch. Do I splice just one of them into the new switch or both? If just one is it the black one or yellow one? Any help or instructions on what I should do will be appreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||BBBAD GT||09-02-2000||09:22 PM||||I don't know about the Hypertech switch, but when I replaced my fan switch with one of Rodney Dickman's, I had a little trouble finding the right switch.  I believe there is only one wire going to this switch, so it sounds like you have the wrong one.  Somebody here can tell you what the one wire looks like, I think green with white stripe or white with green stripe, I'm not sure.  <P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||09-02-2000||09:34 PM||||The 176 degree switch is supposed to be used with the 160 thermostat and the thermo-master chip. A 176 switch will keep your fan running all the time when using either a 180 or 195 'stat||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-03-2000||02:50 PM||||the fan running actually isn't a bad thing.<P>2 wires.... black and yellow..... you got the wrong sensor. That's not a switch. It's the ECM coolant sensor. Cut into that and you have a problem. If you already cut it the wires MUST be soldered back together and waterproofed or the ECM will not read the sensor right. (It may work at first but won't for long.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||09-03-2000||03:10 PM||||...and in case it matters at this point, the (single) wire to the fan switch is green with a white stripe.||||reg||12
Z||000005||Predator||09-03-2000||06:00 PM||||Thanks, and no I haven't cut anything yet that is why I posted here first. Thanks for the info on the color of the correct wire. From that I should be able to find the right sensor. My 1985 GT has 19,000 miles on it and has never been tuned up so I just got done giving it a once over. Man those plugs in the back are tough to get at, but it's done, and it runs a lot better. I am going to try and post some pictures of it here soon. It is completly original except for the few minor things I have done to it. The car has been garaged all it's life and was stored for 12 years before I bought it. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||09-03-2000||06:23 PM||||Just to go into excessive detail (like I always do    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ), if you stand on the passenger side of the car and look at the engine from that side, the fan switch is at about the one- or two-o'clock position from the thermostat housing - it's sticking straight up out of the lower intake manifold.   <P>By the way, 1985 GT with only 19000, always garaged?  You realize you are now <B>morally obligated</B> to post pictures and make us all drool    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-03-2000).]||||reg||10
